      },
      "rev_n_1_aef29a_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_aef29a_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_aef29a",
        "thought_text": "The issue lies in the dynamic programming approach where the state transition formula is incorrect. Specifically, the inner loop should iterate over all possible previous elements (from 1 to j-1) instead of iterating up to `int(N/j)` because we need to ensure the product of any two adjacent elements is at most N. Additionally, the base case and initialization need to be adjusted accordingly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_aef29a_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_ebc61f822a",
        "created_at": 1794503348.1301954
      },
      "rev_n_2_fe390f_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_fe390f_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_fe390f",
        "thought_text": "The issue lies in the dynamic programming approach used to solve the problem. Specifically, the transition state `dp[i][j]` should represent the number of valid sequences of length `i` ending with the element `j`. However, the current implementation incorrectly calculates the number of valid sequences by summing up all previous states without considering the constraint that the product of any two adjacent elements must be at most `N`.\n\nTo fix this, we need to modify the DP table to keep track of the number of valid sequences of length `i` ending with each possible element `j`, ensuring that the product of the last two elements does not exceed `N`. This requires iterating through all possible pairs `(k, j)` where `k * j <= N` and updating the DP table accordingly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_fe390f_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_10d4c560c6",
        "created_at": 1794503396.4611597
      },
      "rev_n_3_7a2dc5_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_7a2dc5_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_7a2dc5",
        "thought_text": "The current approach uses a brute force with memoization strategy, but it incorrectly counts sequences where the product of adjacent elements exceeds N. Instead, we should iterate through all possible values for each position in the sequence and ensure the product constraint is met. This requires a different dynamic programming approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_7a2dc5_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_6202df83a3",
        "created_at": 1794503431.838294
      },
      "rev_n_4_ba73ce_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_ba73ce_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_ba73ce",
        "thought_text": "Revisit the state transition formula to ensure it correctly counts valid sequences based on the product constraint.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-727967750680556550",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794503461.593096
      },
      "rev_n_5_f8cdcb_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_f8cdcb_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_f8cdcb",
        "thought_text": "Optimize the inner loop to avoid unnecessary calculations and improve performance.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-727967750680556550",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794503461.593113
      },
      "rev_n_6_2cec34_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_2cec34_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_2cec34",
        "thought_text": "Implement memoization or caching to store intermediate results and avoid redundant computations.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-727967750680556550",
